      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the technical details.\n\nA rainbow table is a precomputed table for reversing cryptographic hash functions, primarily used to crack password hashes. It works by storing a large number of hash values along with their corresponding plaintext passwords. When a password is hashed, the hash value is looked up in the rainbow table. If a match is found, the corresponding plaintext password is returned.\n\nThe problem with unsalted hashes is that if two users have the same password, their hashes will also be the same. This means that if a rainbow table contains the hash for one user's password, it can be used to crack the password for any other user with the same password. This is why unsalted hashes are vulnerable to rainbow table attacks.\n\nSalting hashes addresses this vulnerability by adding a unique random value, known as a salt, to each password before it is hashed. The salt is then stored alongside the hash. When a password is hashed, the salt is also included in the hash. This means that even if two users have the same password, their salted hashes will be different because the salts are unique.\n\nWhen a rainbow table attack is attempted on salted hashes, the attacker would need to generate a separate rainbow table for each possible",
